 ## The per-session store
 
 - `open(ref)` returns the session's view and keeps the 8 most recently opened sessions; the ninth evicts the least recent, disposing its root and telling the list it closed.
-- An evicted session keeps its latest turn (`retained`: the turn as the view holds it, with the older cursor of the latest-turn read) when it is idle, whole and has no pending prompt, in the same cache. A return to it while its list row's `updatedAt` is unchanged opens a view whose first read takes it as its transcript, so it asks for the row and outline without a page, and shows what a first visit shows: the latest turn, the nav rail and the subagents placed. A changed row, any event for the session or a stream gap drops the kept entry.
+- An evicted session keeps its latest turn (`retained`: the turn as the view holds it, with the older cursor of the latest-turn read) when it is idle, whole and has no pending prompt, in the same cache. A return to it while its list row's `updatedAt` is unchanged opens a view that lands the kept turn and outline as its transcript in the task that opens it, before anything is read, and shows what a first visit shows: the latest turn, the nav rail and the subagents placed. The session's row read still follows, without a page, and only refreshes the row; a refused row read is logged and leaves the kept turn on screen. A changed row, any event for the session or a stream gap drops the kept entry.
 - A session's messages are in the order they were made (`messageOrder`, `transcript/merge.ts`): by the runtime's `time.created`, the id breaking a tie, and every upsert, page merge and latest-turn replace places a message by it. An id says nothing about when its message was made: a task's first prompt (`msg_tasks_…`), a subagent's or a wake's id, or a server-minted `msg_<uuid>` sorts anywhere against the adapter's time-ordered ids. An optimistic stub sorts after every runtime message, because it carries the sender's clock rather than the runtime's and is newer than anything the runtime holds; stubs order among themselves by their send time. The nav rail orders the outline's turns and the loaded ones by the same key (`compareTurnOrder`).
 - A page lands in the conversation as one reactive update: `mergePage` and `replaceLatest` (`transcript/conversation.ts`) batch their writes to messages, parts and text presence, since each write otherwise re-ran the timeline's virtualizer effects and forced a layout (an older page's landing task went from 20-26 ms to 11-14 ms). Each read also lands whole: an older page with its cursor and phase (`older.ts`), the first read's row, page, outline, held events and phase (`snapshot.ts`), a tool part read whole (`part.ts`), a settled turn's messages and parts (`settle.ts`).
 - Every page, the first read's and each older one (`SessionView.loadOlder`, the turns before the older cursor, as tall as the first page), sends every part of its turns, each tool part as its row header (`headerOnly`) unless the reader's shell or edit setting opens its row. Nothing is read after a page lands until the reader acts: opening a fold reads nothing, and opening a header row reads its whole part once (`SessionView.loadPart`). A whole part a later read or reread sends as its header keeps its body, and a newer whole part replaces it (`mergedPart`).
